pulling a fast one on studio executives  in alternate reality justin bieber a  hugely successful rapper who never  actually raps on his own show this is  the untold truth of atlanta you would be  justified in thinking that atlanta was  always an easy sell after all donald  glover was pitching it to fx the same  donald glover who helped define 30 rock  in his first three seasons kept  community alive and had already proven  his ability to write whatever he felt  like and spin it into gold and this was  the same fx that had spent the last  decade proving its ability to sniff out  great comedies like it's always sunny in  philadelphia and archer but according to  glover the only way he could get atlanta  past fx execs was to hide it in a trojan  horse as glover told the press backstage  at the 2016 golden globes after atlanta  took home two of the awards i told fx  the show was something it wasn't until  we got there and then hoped it would be  enjoyable glover never specified exactly  what shape the trojan horse took but  given the popular comedies of the day  it's likely he pitched a show about  nothing glover has said that atlanta was  a longtime dream for him and his brother  steven saying that his brother showed  him a letter he'd sent home while he was  in college that said yo i had this dream  where we write a show together when it  came time to pitch the show glover made  sure that his dream came true hiring  steven as a writer executive producer  and co-showrunner for the show's entire  run since the very first episodes of  atlanta steven's presence on the writing  team has consistently proven to be more  than nepotism on glover's part among  many other gems steven penned the show's  hit paper boy rap as well as season  one's acclaimed exploration into  alternate reality nobody beats the biebs  he and glover made the bizarre and  ultimately genius decision to include  real-life figure justin bieber in the  script cast a black actor in the role  and never address it in the episode as  steven told vulture about their choice  it's a good idea because it makes you  ask yourself questions about the way you  perceive justin bieber this is something  we're never going to get to see unless  we do it ourselves  setting it apart from nearly every show  on television atlanta has an all-black  writing staff for a show determined to  represent black american lives  authentically that type of roster is  especially impactful in fact to anyone  outside the industry it may seem like a  no-brainer but it's hard to overstate  how atypical that decision is in  hollywood in recent years a number of  major studies have attempted to  highlight the problem accurately a  famous color of change report from 2017  found that during the  2016-2017 season  65.4 percent of shows had no black  writers on their staff and another 17.3  percent had only one equally as damning  the report found that overall only 4.8  percent of all employed writers were  black so what percent of writer's rooms  are entirely black a number low enough  for examples to make headlines that's  why glover's choice to only hire black  writers made such a splash and the  choice plays an obvious role in  generating and maintaining the show's  exceptional and sometimes uncomfortable  realism glover has explained the  decision time and again not just  invoking the sincerity it enables but  also the insight at one point telling  vulture i wanted to show white people  you don't know everything about black  culture  is a symptom of the way things are  not the way things necessarily should be  it's no secret that glover moonlights as  a musician he is after all a five-time  grammy-winning hip-hop artist but glover  has always been adamant that his show  about a rapper doesn't star himself as  that rapper why not play a rapper you're  you're a rapper go with what you know  that would have been whack to me  he then went on to explain to colbert  how he felt his pre-existing fame would  undercut the show's underdog every man  objectives glover wanted his show to not  only feel authentic but explore the  meaning of authenticity itself so  casting himself as a poor struggling  rapper on the streets of atlanta would  have rendered any ambitions of  authenticity moods as a successful  showrunner however glover understands  efficiency so he does help write the  show's music on occasion like bieber's  forget about it all night long though  atlantic chronicles the potential rise  of fictional rapper paper boy instead of  the actual rise of real-life rapper  childish gambino the rapper's  fingerprints are nonetheless all over  the show for one thing the show's score  comes from composer ludwig gorensen  perhaps best known for winning an  academy award in grammy for his black  panther soundtrack but who also has  produced and acted as  multi-instrumentalist on every one of  childish gambino's albums the pair met  while both were working on nbc's  community and hit it off due to the  quality of childish gambino's music  which goranson has referred to as such  an inspiration in addition to gorenson  glover chose frequent childish gambino  music video director hero mirai to  direct atlanta the director who filmed  the videos for some of gambino's biggest  hits including this is america had never  actually directed tv before which clever  found useful as glover told vulture  about mariah's unusual background i'm  really glad because when i'd asked him  is this normal for a show he'd be like i  have no idea i don't know but that's how  we made something personal in most  episodes of atlanta it's clear that many  of the characters if not all of them are  high for some like darius it can act as  an important facet of their character or  drive their decisions for others like  earn or van it is just a fact of life a  helpful tool for relieving the stress of  life as a working parent who wants more  but the characters of atlanta aren't the  only ones taking the agile it's cast and  crew art too when interviewed by the new  yorker glover who was high during the  interview revealed that in the writers  room and on the set we do everything  high the effortless chaos of atlanta the  moments of enlightenment followed by an  abrupt return to reality is definitely  shaped by weed when glover ran the  scripts for his pilot episode by fx  executives he was met with a specific  pushback that depending on your  experience and outlook is either  inevitable or unbelievable exacts told  him not to use the n-word in the episode  and when glover disputed the constraint  the execs consented to an odd compromise  you actually said that i had to cool  glover resorted to bringing in a  seasoned executive producer paul sims  who had gained a reputation among black  creators as a white translator helping  explain creators visions and necessities  to execs in a language they can relate  to  eventually glover and sims won out and  atlanta has since included the word  liberally in a manner that the cast and  crew feel is appropriate and accurate  that doesn't stop glover from recalling  the linguistic battle with disbelief  telling the new yorker that i'm black  making a very black show and they're  telling me i can't use the n-word only  in a world run by white people would  that happen  there are few creative teams that have  publicly cited a more diverse set of  inspirations than the team behind  atlanta though perhaps that isn't so  surprising given glover's famously  multifaceted nature and the fact that  neither director mirai nor most of the  writing staff had ever created tv before  the rawness and quirkiness of the  atlanta crew are apparent not just in  their finished product but in their  influences as well mirai has cited louis  and adventure time as structural  inspirations for the show especially how  the latter is very world-based sometimes  the main characters won't even appear in  an episode and the story will sort of  detour with some side character glover  told press that season 2 was inspired by  the format of tiny toon adventures how i  spent my summer vacation in his colbert  interview glover likened atlanta to curb  your enthusiasm for rappers and is also  referred to it as twin peaks for rappers  mirai also cited the cullen brothers  ability to deftly balance multiple tones  glover has cited the albums of kanye  west as inspiration admitting to lining  up his seasons as if they were west's  releases  one of the most memorable entries in the  atlantic canon which is saying a lot in  a show where kat williams plays an  alligator man and characters identify as  trans-racial is the season 2 episode  teddy perkins the episode sees darius  trapped in a dimly lit decrepit mansion  with an unsettling vaguely threatening  old man apparently born black but now  white due to an extraordinary amount of  plastic surgery the old man perkins  looks just a touch inhuman right in the  uncanny valley sweet spot his slow  movement and quiet forced speech bring  the episode as close to an actual horror  movie as atlanta had ever ventured up to  that point  wasn't it  go ahead  walk in  perkins was played by glover himself who  went all in on the role staying in  character as perkins for the duration of  filming actor derek haywood who played  perkins elderly brother revealed that  perkins true identity was kept secret  even to fellow actors he told vulture i  was introduced to him the night before  at the fitting but i didn't know it was  donald and obviously he didn't want you  to know  in 2017 atlanta won the golden globe for  best television series musical or comedy  and glover won for best actor in a  television series musical or comedy then  at that year's emmy awards the momentum  continued glover won emmys for  outstanding lead actor in a comedy  series and outstanding directing for a  comedy series the wave of success that  atlanta and glover were riding in  response to their first season was  tremendous and it was also historic  glover's win for directing was the first  time a black person had ever won an emmy  for directing comedy of any kind and as  the los angeles times noted glover was  the first director to win for a show in  which they acted since alan alda won in  1977 awards were not the only bragging  point for atlanta as its pilot set a new  viewership record among basic cable  comedies and it managed to maintain the  majority of its audience throughout its  run when all its rating success is  combined with the peabody it received  for its seamless blend of vibrant  character study and rich socio-political  commentary it becomes clear that atlanta  is more than just your average tv show  fans and critics can't get enough of  atlanta but its creator can despite the  series stellar fan following critical  reception and cultural importance glover  has repeatedly made it clear that  atlanta won't run for long as early as  2018 he was quoted as saying atlanta  won't interest me much longer fx later  made that prediction official announcing  that season 4 would be the last for  atlanta glover explained the decision to  reporters with a lengthy paragraph that  can be easily boiled down to its opening  sentence death is natural as  disappointed as many fans had to have  been when they heard the news those who  have followed glover's career might have  expected an early finale glover is  famous for his short attention span  which caused him to quickly exit 30 rock  and last only slightly longer on  community as former co-star danny poody  recalled glover's talent was so obvious  that his cast mates knew he would move  on almost immediately it's worth  remembering that short attention spans  swing both ways however and the same  restless drive that causes glover to end  projects can easily push him to revisit  them as he told reporters shortly after  his death is natural speech if there's a  reason to come back to atlanta of course  it always depends i like keeping my  options open  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite shows are coming soon  subscribe to our youtube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one  [Music]  you

